    "difficulty": "Development",
    "difficultyReason": "Parts a and b are routine: differentiate 5/x, justify the sign using x^2>0, and evaluate two values. The hardest demand is part c, where the student must see that a negative derivative only compares values within one interval, and that -2 and 1 lie on opposite sides of the asymptote x=0. That is a real interpretation step. It is more scaffolded than p17-q14, because part b has already produced the two values and part c asks a direct yes/no question rather than asking the student to explain an anomaly. It is harder than the routine stationary-point work in p13-practice.",
