  "content": {
    "id": "v2-4-practice-section-j2-075135-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{5}{x}$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-4-practice-section-j2-075135-j2-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Differentiate $f(x)$ and show that $f'(x)<0$ for all $x$ in its domain.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=-\\dfrac{5}{x^2}<0$ for every $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=5x^{-1}\\\\f'(x)&=-5x^{-2}\\\\&=-\\frac{5}{x^2}\\end{align*}$$\nFor every $x\\ne0$, $x^2>0$, so $f'(x)<0$ throughout its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-4-practice-section-j2-075135-j2-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find $f(-2)$ and $f(1)$. Which is greater?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(-2)=-\\dfrac52$, $f(1)=5$; $f(1)$ is greater.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-2)&=\\frac{5}{-2}=-\\frac52\\\\f(1)&=\\frac51=5\\end{align*}$$\nSo $f(1)>f(-2)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-4-practice-section-j2-075135-j2-q-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Does $f'(x)<0$ show that $f(-2)>f(1)$? Explain.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. $f$ is decreasing on $x<0$ and on $x>0$ separately; $-2$ and $1$ lie on opposite sides of the asymptote $x=0$.",
          "worked": "The domain excludes $x=0$ (a vertical asymptote), so it splits into two intervals, $x<0$ and $x>0$. $f'(x)<0$ shows that $f$ decreases on each interval separately. It only compares values within one interval. Since $-2<0<1$, the two values lie on different branches, so $f'(x)<0$ says nothing about $f(-2)$ against $f(1)$. Part b confirms $f(-2)<f(1)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
